[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'People ask me all the time if I\'m worried that AI is going to replace people like me. Honestly, not at all. AI can generate words. It can generate images, maybe even videos, but it can\'t replace a real person. It doesn\'t have life experience. [music] It doesn\'t have'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'genuine emotion. It doesn\'t know what it\'s like to struggle, to learn, [music] or to grow. And no matter how advanced it gets, it\'ll never be able to sit in front of a camera, have a real conversation, and convince people it\'s'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'human. That\'s Tina. Her face, her voice, every word, 100% AI, and she just sat there telling you that could never happen. But here\'s the fun part. She\'s not the only'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'AI in this video. Hi, I\'m Kira. In this video, I\'ll show you exactly how I built Tina from scratch. a consistent face, a consistent voice, and a full long- form talking video from a single prompt. No'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'manually splitting dialogue, no rewriting prompts for every line. We\'ll begin by creating our AI character from scratch. There are several ways to create AI characters. You can start with an existing avatar, generate one from a'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompt, or use dedicated character creation tools. I\'ll quickly show you a couple of options before creating ours. The first one is Google Flow. Inside a new project, open the character section.'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'These presets generate different character prompts that you can use as a starting point. You can edit them or write your own prompt from scratch before generating the image.'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The second option is Higsfield. Go to the image section and select AI influencer. Here you can choose things like the character type, gender, ethnicity, age, and lots of other details. Instead of generating a prompt,'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Higsfield generates the character images directly based on the options you select. It\'s a very quick way to explore different character ideas. For this tutorial, though, I\'ll create the character from a prompt, so you can use'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the same workflow with almost any image generation model. If you don\'t have Higsfield, I also made a free character prompt generator using Claude. It asks a few questions about your character and'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generates a detailed prompt for you. The link is in the description and that\'s what I\'ll be using here. I\'ll choose the characteristics I want for Tina, then click generate prompt. Once the'

[02:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompt is ready, I\'ll copy it into Google Flow using Nano Banana Pro. You can also use Chat GPT, Cream, or any other image generation model. Here\'s the first result. It\'s a good starting'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'point, but I almost never use the very first generation. Small differences in expression or facial features can make one version feel much more natural than another. Let\'s generate another one. Generating a few variations gives you'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'options to choose from, and it\'s much easier to pick the strongest image than trying to perfect the first one. Let\'s try one last time. I like this one the best, so I\'ll use'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this as Tina. Now that we have our character, let\'s place her in a setting that matches her role. Since I want her to be an interview style presenter, I want an interview background. I found this'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reference image on Pinterest. I\'ll upload it to chat GPT and ask it to describe the setting as a prompt. Then I\'ll paste that prompt into Google Flow along with Tina\'s image to generate the'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'final scene. I also tried generating the same scene in chat GPT. I actually prefer chat GPT version, so I\'ll use it instead. Before we move on, there\'s one more step that I\'d highly recommend.'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Most AI generated portraits have a slightly plastic looking skin texture. To make the skin look more natural, I like to run the image through a skin enhancement or upscaling tool. I\'m using Topaz Bloom, but you can use any similar'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tool that enhances skin details. Here\'s the original image, and here\'s the enhanced version. If you look closely, you can see a lot more natural skin texture.'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It doesn\'t look as smooth or artificial anymore, and I think it makes the character feel much more realistic. It\'s a small change, but when your presenter is going to be on screen for several minutes, little details like this really'

[04:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'add up. Once that\'s finished, I\'ll download the image and go back to character panel inside Google Flow. This time, instead of generating a character with a prompt, I\'ll upload the finished image.'

[04:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now I can choose a voice, customize how I want her to speak, give her a name, and that\'s it. Our character is ready. Now it\'s time to'

[04:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make our character talk. I\'ll be generating all the videos inside Google Flow using the Gemini Omni Flash model. Right now, it can generate a maximum of 10 seconds of video at a time. Normally'

[04:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that means splitting a long dialogue into multiple sections and writing a separate prompt for every generation. To avoid that, I created a single master prompt that works with Google Flow'

[05:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Agent. Instead of writing prompts for every 10-second clip, I only need to provide the complete dialogue once, and the agent takes care of generating the entire sequence. This is the master prompt I\'ll be using. If you\'d like to'

[05:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use the same one, you\'ll find it in the description. The first thing you need to do is tag your character by typing at and selecting it. This tells Google Flow exactly which character should perform the dialogue. Right below that, simply'

[05:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'paste your complete dialogue. Make sure it\'s exactly how you want it to be spoken. Next comes the instruction section. Here you can customize the dialogue delivery style. For example, you can ask the character to speak'

[05:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gently with a smile at a fast conversational pace or in a serious interview style. This has a big impact on how the final performance feels. The prompt also tells Google Flow Agent to automatically split your long dialogue'

[06:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into consecutive 10-second segments while preserving every word in the correct order. That way, you don\'t have to manually break the script into smaller pieces yourself. The next section is visual consistency. This'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tells the model to keep the character\'s appearance and overall scene consistent across every generated segment. Finally, there\'s the camera direction. Here you can decide how you want the shots to look. By default, I use two static'

[06:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera shots in each segment to make the interview feel more natural, but you can easily change this. If you prefer a single front-facing shot throughout the entire video, just edit the section accordingly.'

[06:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All right, let\'s generate it. First, I\'ll copy the master prompt into Google Flow and tag my character. One more thing, under prompt settings,'

[06:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can choose whether Google Flow Agent asks for permission before generating each clip. I usually turn that off so it runs through the entire workflow automatically.'

[07:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now I\'ll click generate. Within a few seconds, all the clips are ready. Let\'s see how they turned out. People ask me all the time if I\'m'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'worried that AI is going to replace people like me. Honestly, [music] not at all. AI can generate words. It can generate images. Maybe even videos. But it can\'t replace a real person. It'

[07:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'doesn\'t have life experience. It doesn\'t have genuine emotion. It doesn\'t know what it\'s like to struggle, to learn, or to grow. And no matter how advanced it gets,'

[07:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'ll never be able to sit in front of a camera, have a real conversation, and convince people it\'s human. That\'s why I\'m not worried. Not even a little. I love that an AI is arguing against AI.'

[08:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Anyway, the results look great, but I do want to be honest with you. It doesn\'t work perfectly every single time. Most of the time, Google Flow gets the dialogue exactly right, but occasionally'

[08:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it might repeat a word, skip a word, or make a small mistake in one of the generated clips. The good news is that it\'s usually easy to fix. Just open the clip with the error, click reuse prompt, and generate it again. In most cases,'

[08:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the problem is corrected on the next generation. If it still isn\'t right, you can edit the prompt, reduce the amount of dialogue if that particular section is too long, and generate it again. Once all the clips are ready, it\'s time to'

[08:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'put them together. You can actually do this inside Google Flow by creating a scene and adding each generated clip in sequence. From there, you can preview the entire'

[08:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'performance and export it. Personally, I prefer doing this in a video editor because it gives me much more control. I\'m using Da Vinci Resolve. I simply import all the'

[09:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generated clips, arrange them in the correct order, and trim any long pauses or dead silence between them. [clears throat] Once the edit is finished, I export the video. But there\'s one more thing we should talk'

[09:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about. Voice consistency. If your entire video is made only from the clips generated in Google Flow, you can skip this step because the voice will already be consistent. However, if you want to add extra dialogue that wasn\'t generated'

[09:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in Flow, or if you want your presenter to speak in your own voice or another custom voice, you\'ll need an AI voice cloning tool like 11 Labs. I\'ll show you both workflows. First, export your'

[09:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'edited video and open 11 Labs. The first thing you\'ll need is one consistent voice. You can either choose one from the voices library or create your own. To create a new voice, open voices and select create voice.'

[10:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Choose the voice cloning option. Then upload an audio or video sample of the voice you want to clone. This could be your own voice or even the voice generated by Google Flow. After uploading it, give the voice a name and'

[10:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'save it. Now open the voice changer tool. Upload the video or audio whose voice you want to replace. Select the cloned voice and generate the new audio.'

[10:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you need to create completely new dialogue in the same voice, open text to speech, paste your script, select the same cloned voice, and'

[10:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generate the audio. The delivery style won\'t be an exact match to the original Google Flow voice. You\'ll probably notice small differences in pacing, tone, or expression, but for most use cases, I think it\'s good'

[10:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'enough. Finally, go back to your video editor and replace the original audio with the cloned version. And that\'s it. From here, you can do any final polish inside your video editor, like improving the color or hiding watermarks using a'

[11:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mask. I\'m not going to cover those steps because they\'re different in every editing software, but you can easily find tutorials on YouTube for whichever editor you\'re using. And that\'s the'

[11:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'complete workflow I use to create long- form AI presenters with Google Flow Agent. It\'s not a perfect workflow yet, and I\'m sure it\'ll keep improving as these models get better. But even in its'

[11:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'current state, I think it\'s a really practical way to create AI presenters with consistent characters, voices, and long- form dialogue. I hope you found this tutorial helpful. If you have any'

